## Open-Source Bridge and Developer Ecosystem

- Playgama develops Bridge in public as an open-source, unified SDK for publishing HTML5 games across supported platforms with one integration.
- Bridge handles platform-specific differences through a common API for advertising, storage, payments, leaderboards, and other supported capabilities. It is designed to reduce the need to build and maintain a separate integration for every destination.
- Playgama publishes the core SDK, engine integrations, examples, documentation, versioned releases, migration guides, and related tools. This shared infrastructure is a concrete contribution to the HTML5 game-development ecosystem: developers can inspect and adapt the implementation under the applicable licenses instead of rebuilding the same platform integrations from scratch.
- Playgama also supports the developer community through public documentation, manual QA feedback, [Discord](https://discord.gg/pzqd2upxr8), and [Telegram](https://t.me/playgama_bridge), where developers can ask questions, exchange experience, and learn from each other.

## Web Gaming Research and Industry Development

- Since 2025, Playgama has published a series of original studies intended to make the fragmented web-gaming market easier to understand and navigate.
- [Web-based Game Engine Rankings](https://wiki.playgama.com/playgama/articles/introducing-web-based-game-engine-rankings-first-issue-h1-2025) analyzes public data about new HTML5 releases to track engine shares, technology adoption, and fast-growing tools. A [follow-up study by game category](https://wiki.playgama.com/playgama/articles/unitys-dominance-challenged-in-web-gaming.-playgama.com-maps-top-engines-across-web-game-categories) shows how engine use differs across genres.
- The [Web Games Industry Market Map](https://playgama.com/blog/2026/07/27/playgama-publishes-web-games-market-map-covering-120-companies/) describes 120+ companies across 13 domains, including engines, platforms, distributors, analytics, monetization, in-game payments, intrinsic advertising, and cloud hosting. It is a representative industry resource, not an exhaustive directory or ranking.
- Together with open-source tooling, developer education, advertising technology, distribution products, and partner integrations, these publications are part of Playgama's broader contribution to the web-gaming industry.

## Game Review and Quality Assurance

- No game enters the Playgama catalog or partner distribution before it passes manual moderation and testing. A public sandbox link published through Playgama MCP is not catalog publication. Human reviewers play the game and assess technical operation as well as the quality of the player experience.
- Review covers bugs and core behavior, including controls, loading, saves, audio, advertising, and required menus or features. Reviewers also check translations, build size, and loading performance where relevant.
- Games are tested across relevant device types, resolutions, and screen formats. Playgama gives developers actionable feedback and re-tests games after material fixes.
- Review considers distribution-platform requirements and apparent intellectual-property risks. Obvious clones and games without sufficient distinct value for players are not accepted.
- Use of AI in production is not itself a reason for rejection; the finished game must meet the same experience and quality requirements as any other submission.

